  "id": "COMPOUND:38392 - PROTEIN:6231",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence strongly suggests a mechanistic link between the compound IYGRWBQEGQIQRP-UHFFFAOYSA-N and the histone methyltransferase enzyme Histone-lysine N-methyltransferase, H3 lysine-9 specific 5. This relationship is supported by multiple pathways, all of which point to the enzyme as a target. The compound is identified as a target of several compounds, including Kuhlmannin and 2-Carbomethoxy-9,10-anthraquinone, which are known to interact with the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. This complex is a key component of the polycomb repressive complex 2 (PRC2), which is involved in epigenetic regulation and is associated with various diseases, including cancer. The presence of multiple targets in the pathway indicates a potential role for IYGRWBQEGQIQRP-UHFFFAOYSA-N in modulating the activity of this complex, thereby influencing histone methylation patterns. The evidence is consistent with a mechanism involving the inhibition of the PRC2 complex, which is a well-documented pathway in cancer biology. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that IYGRWBQEGQIQRP-UHFFFAOYSA-N may act as a modulator of the PRC2 complex, potentially through direct interaction with its components.",
